<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-trig-integration">
    <title>Trigonometric Integration</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-trig-integrals-sin-cos">
        <title>Powers of Sine and Cosine</title>

        <p>
            We now consider integrals of the form
        </p>

        <me>
            \int \sin^m(x)\cos^n(x)\,dx.
        </me>

        <p>
            The appropriate strategy depends on whether the powers
            <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are odd or even.
        </p>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-trig-identities">
            <title>Useful Trigonometric Identities</title>

            <p>
                The Pythagorean identity is
            </p>

            <me>
                \sin^2x+\cos^2x=1.
            </me>

            <p>
                This allows us to write
            </p>

            <me>
                \sin^2x=1-\cos^2x
            </me>

            <p>
                or
            </p>

            <me>
                \cos^2x=1-\sin^2x.
            </me>

            <p>
                When both powers are even, the power-reduction identities are
                useful:
            </p>

            <me>
                \cos^2x
                =
                \frac{1}{2}(1+\cos 2x),
            </me>

            <me>
                \sin^2x
                =
                \frac{1}{2}(1-\cos 2x).
            </me>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-sine-odd">
            <title>Case 1: The Power of Sine Is Odd</title>

            <p>
                If the power of sine is odd, save one factor of
                <m>\sin x</m>. Convert the remaining even power of sine
                to cosine using
            </p>

            <me>
                \sin^2x=1-\cos^2x,
            </me>

            <p>
                and then use the substitution <m>u=\cos x</m>.
            </p>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-sine-odd">
                <title>An odd power of sine</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^7x\cos^2x\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Save one factor of <m>\sin x</m>:
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sin^7x
                        =
                        \sin^6x\sin x
                        =
                        (\sin^2x)^3\sin x.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^7x\cos^2x\,dx
                        =
                        \int
                        (1-\cos^2x)^3
                        \cos^2x
                        \sin x\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        u=\cos x,
                        \qquad
                        du=-\sin x\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Then
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        -\int(1-u^2)^3u^2\,du.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Expanding,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        -\int
                        \left(
                        u^2-3u^4+3u^6-u^8
                        \right)\,du.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Thus,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        -\frac{1}{3}u^3
                        +
                        \frac{3}{5}u^5
                        -
                        \frac{3}{7}u^7
                        +
                        \frac{1}{9}u^9
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Substituting back,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^7x\cos^2x\,dx
                        =
                        -\frac{1}{3}\cos^3x
                        +
                        \frac{3}{5}\cos^5x
                        -
                        \frac{3}{7}\cos^7x
                        +
                        \frac{1}{9}\cos^9x
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-cosine-odd">
            <title>Case 2: The Power of Cosine Is Odd</title>

            <p>
                If the power of cosine is odd, save one factor of
                <m>\cos x</m>. Convert the remaining even power of cosine
                using
            </p>

            <me>
                \cos^2x=1-\sin^2x,
            </me>

            <p>
                and then use <m>u=\sin x</m>.
            </p>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-cosine-odd">
                <title>An odd power of cosine</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^{100}x\cos^3x\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Write
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \cos^3x
                        =
                        \cos^2x\cos x.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^{100}x\cos^3x\,dx
                        =
                        \int
                        \sin^{100}x
                        (1-\sin^2x)
                        \cos x\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        u=\sin x,
                        \qquad
                        du=\cos x\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Then
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int u^{100}(1-u^2)\,du
                        =
                        \int
                        \left(
                        u^{100}-u^{102}
                        \right)\,du.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^{100}x\cos^3x\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{\sin^{101}x}{101}
                        -
                        \frac{\sin^{103}x}{103}
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-sine-cosine-even">
            <title>Case 3: Both Powers Are Even</title>

            <p>
                If both powers are even, use the power-reduction identities
                to reduce the powers.
            </p>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-both-even">
                <title>Even powers of sine and cosine</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^2x\cos^2x\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Use the power-reduction identities:
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sin^2x\cos^2x
                        =
                        \frac{1}{4}
                        (1-\cos 2x)(1+\cos 2x).
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sin^2x\cos^2x
                        =
                        \frac{1}{4}
                        \left(
                        1-\cos^2 2x
                        \right).
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Apply the power-reduction identity again:
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \cos^2 2x
                        =
                        \frac{1}{2}(1+\cos 4x).
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^2x\cos^2x\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{1}{8}
                        \int
                        (1-\cos 4x)\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\sin^2x\cos^2x\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{x}{8}
                        -
                        \frac{1}{32}\sin 4x
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>

            <remark xml:id="rem-both-odd">
                <title>If both powers are odd</title>

                <p>
                    If both powers are odd, either of the first two strategies
                    can be used. It is often easiest to save a factor from the
                    function having the smaller power.
                </p>
            </remark>
        </subsection>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-trig-integrals-tan-sec">
        <title>Powers of Tangent and Secant</title>

        <p>
            We next consider integrals of the form
        </p>

        <me>
            \int\tan^m(x)\sec^n(x)\,dx.
        </me>

        <p>
            Two identities and two derivatives are particularly useful:
        </p>

        <me>
            1+\tan^2x=\sec^2x,
        </me>

        <me>
            \frac{d}{dx}(\tan x)=\sec^2x,
        </me>

        <me>
            \frac{d}{dx}(\sec x)=\sec x\tan x.
        </me>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-secant-even">
            <title>Case 1: The Power of Secant Is Even</title>

            <p>
                If the power of secant is even, save one factor of
                <m>\sec^2x</m> and use
            </p>

            <me>
                u=\tan x.
            </me>

            <p>
                Convert the remaining powers of secant using
                <m>\sec^2x=1+\tan^2x</m>.
            </p>

            <example xml:id="ex-secant-even">
                <title>An even power of secant</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\tan^2x\sec^6x\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Save a factor of <m>\sec^2x</m>:
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int
                        \tan^2x
                        \sec^4x
                        \sec^2x\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Since
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sec^4x=(1+\tan^2x)^2,
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        u=\tan x,
                        \qquad
                        du=\sec^2x\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        The integral becomes
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int u^2(1+u^2)^2\,du.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Expanding,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int
                        \left(
                        u^6+2u^4+u^2
                        \right)\,du.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\tan^2x\sec^6x\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{1}{7}\tan^7x
                        +
                        \frac{2}{5}\tan^5x
                        +
                        \frac{1}{3}\tan^3x
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-tangent-odd">
            <title>Case 2: The Power of Tangent Is Odd</title>

            <p>
                If the power of tangent is odd, save a factor of
                <m>\sec x\tan x</m> and use
            </p>

            <me>
                u=\sec x.
            </me>

            <p>
                Convert the remaining even powers of tangent using
            </p>

            <me>
                \tan^2x=\sec^2x-1.
            </me>

            <example xml:id="ex-tangent-odd">
                <title>An odd power of tangent</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\tan^3x\sec^4x\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Save a factor of <m>\sec x\tan x</m>:
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int
                        \tan^2x
                        \sec^3x
                        (\sec x\tan x)\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Use
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \tan^2x=\sec^2x-1
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        and let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        u=\sec x,
                        \qquad
                        du=\sec x\tan x\,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Then
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int
                        (u^2-1)u^3\,du
                        =
                        \int
                        (u^5-u^3)\,du.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\tan^3x\sec^4x\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{1}{6}\sec^6x
                        -
                        \frac{1}{4}\sec^4x
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>

            <remark xml:id="rem-tan-sec-both">
                <title>When both strategies apply</title>

                <p>
                    If the tangent power is odd and the secant power is even,
                    either strategy may work. It is often faster to choose the
                    substitution that removes the higher power.
                </p>
            </remark>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-tan-sec-hard-case">
            <title>When Neither Simple Strategy Applies</title>

            <p>
                If the power of secant is odd while the power of tangent is
                even, neither of the simple substitution strategies applies
                directly.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example,
            </p>

            <me>
                \int\tan^2x\sec^3x\,dx
                =
                \int\sec^5x\,dx
                -
                \int\sec^3x\,dx.
            </me>

            <p>
                Integrals involving odd powers of secant can require
                integration by parts.
            </p>
        </subsection>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-trig-substitution">
        <title>Trigonometric Substitution</title>

        <p>
            Some integrals containing square roots cannot be simplified by an
            ordinary substitution. Trigonometric substitutions are designed
            to use identities such as
        </p>

        <me>
            1-\sin^2\theta=\cos^2\theta
        </me>

        <p>
            and
        </p>

        <me>
            1+\tan^2\theta=\sec^2\theta
        </me>

        <p>
            to remove the square root.
        </p>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-trig-sub-a2-minus-x2">
            <title>Integrands Containing <m>\sqrt{a^2-x^2}</m></title>

            <p>
                For an expression containing
            </p>

            <me>
                \sqrt{a^2-x^2},
            </me>

            <p>
                use the substitution
            </p>

            <me>
                x=a\sin\theta.
            </me>

            <p>
                Then
            </p>

            <me>
                \sqrt{a^2-x^2}
                =
                a\sqrt{1-\sin^2\theta}
                =
                a|\cos\theta|.
            </me>

            <p>
                We choose the range of <m>\theta</m> so that the sign of
                <m>\cos\theta</m> is known.
            </p>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-sub-semicircle">
                <title>A definite integral using trigonometric substitution</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int_{-3}^{3}\sqrt{9-x^2}\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        x=3\sin\theta,
                        \qquad
                        dx=3\cos\theta\,d\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        The limits become
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        x=-3
                        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                        \theta=-\frac{\pi}{2},
                    </me>

                    <me>
                        x=3
                        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                        \theta=\frac{\pi}{2}.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Also,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sqrt{9-x^2}
                        =
                        3\cos\theta
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        on this interval. Thus,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int_{-3}^{3}\sqrt{9-x^2}\,dx
                        =
                        9
                        \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}
                        \cos^2\theta\,d\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Using the power-reduction identity,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \cos^2\theta
                        =
                        \frac{1}{2}(1+\cos2\theta),
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        we obtain
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int_{-3}^{3}\sqrt{9-x^2}\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{9\pi}{2}.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-sub-inverse-sine">
                <title>An antiderivative involving inverse sine</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{25-x^2}}\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        x=5\sin\theta,
                        \qquad
                        dx=5\cos\theta\,d\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Then
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sqrt{25-x^2}
                        =
                        5\cos\theta
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        on the chosen range. Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int
                        \frac{1}{\sqrt{25-x^2}}\,dx
                        =
                        \int d\theta
                        =
                        \theta+C.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Since
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sin\theta=\frac{x}{5},
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        we have
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \theta
                        =
                        \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{5}\right).
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int
                        \frac{1}{\sqrt{25-x^2}}\,dx
                        =
                        \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{5}\right)+C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-trig-sub-a2-plus-x2">
            <title>Integrands Containing <m>\sqrt{a^2+x^2}</m></title>

            <p>
                For an expression containing
            </p>

            <me>
                \sqrt{a^2+x^2},
            </me>

            <p>
                use
            </p>

            <me>
                x=a\tan\theta.
            </me>

            <p>
                Then
            </p>

            <me>
                \sqrt{a^2+x^2}
                =
                a\sqrt{1+\tan^2\theta}
                =
                a|\sec\theta|.
            </me>

            <p>
                A useful antiderivative is
            </p>

            <me>
                \int\sec\theta\,d\theta
                =
                \ln|\sec\theta+\tan\theta|+C.
            </me>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-sub-plus">
                <title>A square root of <m>a^2+x^2</m></title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{5+x^2}}\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        x=\sqrt{5}\tan\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Then
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        dx=\sqrt{5}\sec^2\theta\,d\theta
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        and
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sqrt{5+x^2}
                        =
                        \sqrt{5}\sec\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{5+x^2}}\,dx
                        =
                        \int\sec\theta\,d\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{5+x^2}}\,dx
                        =
                        \ln|\sec\theta+\tan\theta|+C.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        From
                        <m>\tan\theta=x/\sqrt{5}</m>, a right triangle gives
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sec\theta
                        =
                        \frac{\sqrt{x^2+5}}{\sqrt{5}}.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Thus,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{5+x^2}}\,dx
                        =
                        \ln
                        \left|
                        x+\sqrt{x^2+5}
                        \right|
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-trig-sub-x2-minus-a2">
            <title>Integrands Containing <m>\sqrt{x^2-a^2}</m></title>

            <p>
                For an expression containing
            </p>

            <me>
                \sqrt{x^2-a^2},
            </me>

            <p>
                use
            </p>

            <me>
                x=a\sec\theta.
            </me>

            <p>
                Then
            </p>

            <me>
                \sqrt{x^2-a^2}
                =
                a\sqrt{\sec^2\theta-1}
                =
                a|\tan\theta|.
            </me>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-sub-x2-minus">
                <title>A square root of <m>x^2-a^2</m></title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-4}}\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Let
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        x=2\sec\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Then
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        dx=2\sec\theta\tan\theta\,d\theta
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        and
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sqrt{x^2-4}
                        =
                        2\tan\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-4}}\,dx
                        =
                        \int\sec\theta\,d\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Hence,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-4}}\,dx
                        =
                        \ln|\sec\theta+\tan\theta|+C.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Since
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sec\theta=\frac{x}{2}
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        and
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \tan\theta
                        =
                        \frac{\sqrt{x^2-4}}{2},
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        the constant factor inside the logarithm can be
                        absorbed into the constant of integration. Thus,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-4}}\,dx
                        =
                        \ln
                        \left|
                        x+\sqrt{x^2-4}
                        \right|
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-sub-rearrange">
                <title>Rearranging before trigonometric substitution</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{4x^2-25}}\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        First rewrite the square root:
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \sqrt{4x^2-25}
                        =
                        2\sqrt{x^2-\frac{25}{4}}.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{4x^2-25}}\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{1}{2}
                        \int
                        \frac{1}{
                        \sqrt{x^2-\frac{25}{4}}
                        }
                        \,dx.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Use
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        x=\frac{5}{2}\sec\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Following the same procedure as in the previous
                        example gives
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{\sqrt{4x^2-25}}\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{1}{2}
                        \ln
                        \left|
                        2x+\sqrt{4x^2-25}
                        \right|
                        +
                        C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-trig-sub-summary">
            <title>Choosing a Trigonometric Substitution</title>

            <p>
                The three standard substitutions are:
            </p>

            <me>
                \sqrt{a^2-x^2}
                \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                x=a\sin\theta,
            </me>

            <me>
                \sqrt{a^2+x^2}
                \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                x=a\tan\theta,
            </me>

            <me>
                \sqrt{x^2-a^2}
                \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                x=a\sec\theta.
            </me>

            <p>
                Sometimes an expression must first be rearranged or factored
                before one of these forms becomes apparent.
            </p>

            <example xml:id="ex-trig-sub-arctan">
                <title>A related tangent substitution</title>

                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{1+4x^2}\,dx.
                    </me>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Rewrite
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        1+4x^2
                        =
                        4\left(
                        \frac{1}{4}+x^2
                        \right).
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Use
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        x=\frac{1}{2}\tan\theta,
                        \qquad
                        dx=\frac{1}{2}\sec^2\theta\,d\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Then
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        1+4x^2
                        =
                        1+\tan^2\theta
                        =
                        \sec^2\theta.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{1+4x^2}\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{1}{2}
                        \int d\theta
                        =
                        \frac{1}{2}\theta+C.
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        Since
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \theta=\tan^{-1}(2x),
                    </me>

                    <p>
                        we obtain
                    </p>

                    <me>
                        \int\frac{1}{1+4x^2}\,dx
                        =
                        \frac{1}{2}\tan^{-1}(2x)+C.
                    </me>
                </solution>
            </example>
        </subsection>

    </section>

</chapter>